        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The Mir 24 crew, Commander Anatoly Soloviev and Flight  Engineer Pavel Vinogradov, launched in a Soyuz TM-26 spacecraft from the Baikonur Cosmodrome in Kazakhstan on August 5 to replace the Russian Mir 23 crew on board the Mir Space Station.  The main objective of the Mir 24 mission was to repair the damage caused by the collision of a Progress resupply ship during the Mir 23 mission.<P>Many extravehicular activities (EVAs) were performed, two of which were internal, in an attempt to repair the damaged Spektr Module.  One spacewalk was performed inside the depressurized module to reattach power cables from the solar arrays to the rest of Mir.  A second spacewalk was performed to determine the location of holes caused by the collision, which caused the oxygen leak.  During the third spacewalk, the crew unsuccessfully tried to reconnect and reactivate the damaged solar panels on the Spektr Module.  The damaged solar arrays were dismantled during a later EVA on November 3, 1997, and a new solar array was installed on the Kvant Module on November 6, 1997.<P>On September 27, 1997, the Space Shuttle Atlantis (STS-86) brought the NASA 6 crewmember to replace the NASA 5 crewmember on board the station.  Science conducted on Mir during the Mir 24 mission was sparse.  However, research continured after the repairs were made and the NASA 6 crewmember arrived.  Research areas included human life sciences, fundamental biology to study animals and plants, biotechnology, materials sciences, and spacecraft structural dynamics and environment. Results assisted researchers in developing the operating procedures on board the International Space Station (ISS).<P>On January 24, 1998, the Space Shuttle Endeavour (STS-89) docked with Mir to deliver the NASA 7 crewmember and return the NASA 6 crewmember to Earth.  During the five days of joint U.S./Russian operations, astronauts and cosmonauts transferred experiment hardware, science samples, and data collected by the Mir 24/NASA 6 crew from Mir to the Shuttle where they were returned to Earth to be analyzed by researchers.  Crewmembers also transferred water, food, hardware, and other supplies to Mir to support the health and well-being of the current crew and science investigations on the Mir Station.  <P>On January 29, 1998, the Russian Mir 25 crew, who consisted of one French and two Russian cosmonauts, arrived at the Mir Space Station to replace the Mir 24 crew.  On February 19, 1998, the Mir 24 crew returned to Earth with the French cosmonaut. <END>
        </div>

